WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.430
Film Courage:&nbsp; Storymaker?&nbsp;

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:02.430
What is this?

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:06.959
Cecilia Najar:&nbsp; Storymaker was borne out
of a land of blocked writers and it’s a

00:00:06.959 --> 00:00:16.920
90 minute process that gives you prompts basically
to develop a character-driven story based

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:17.920
on anything.&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:26.530
It can take pickles, airplanes, hotels, etc.&nbsp;

00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:32.270
Because we live in stories, we watch stories,
we’re so immersed in storytelling, it’s

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.280
in our DNA.&nbsp;

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:42.219
If you can stop thinking and just allow your
reaction to go forward, you will develop a

00:00:42.219 --> 00:00:43.219
story.&nbsp;

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:48.379
I’m not saying it will be a great story,
but it will have a beginning, middle and end.&nbsp;

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:56.999
It will have characters that change and dilemmas
that will be interesting, an a genre that

00:00:56.999 --> 00:00:58.649
could attract an audience.&nbsp;

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:08.390
These simple prompts, when you start allowing
your first reactions to come forward, you’ll

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:15.640
start to feel how powerful your internal storytelling
clock is and have much more confidence in

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:16.640
it.&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.920
That is the reason why I came up with this
process because the other thing is, as writers

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.340
we practice dialogue, we practice scenes,
right?&nbsp;

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:33.969
We practice all these things, but no one practices
the art of telling an entire story.&nbsp;

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:39.370
So if I can get you to tell an entire story
in 90 minutes with a beginning, middle and

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:47.249
end, good or bad (we don’t know), you could
do that once a week, which means you could

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:50.250
have 52 stories at the end of the year.&nbsp;

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:55.640
And how many good stories do you think you’d
have if you had 52 stories every year?&nbsp;

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.929
Probably more than you have now.&nbsp;

00:01:57.929 --> 00:02:04.429
And you would also be building an incredible
muscle of developing ideas, of being able

00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:10.280
to solve story problems, of knowing how characters
change, of knowing your genres, about knowing

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:16.030
your ups-and-downs of the rhythm of the story,
and it would just become part of you and you

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:17.350
wouldn’t even have to think about it.&nbsp;

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:22.780
So that’s my goal for writers, to really
learn this as a dance (like you would learn

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.810
the tango) and learn to dance this story without
even thinking.

